# 7. IDENTITY, AUTHENTICATION AND AUTHORIZATION

Firebase Authentication is the shared identity foundation.

Each portal must use a consistent identity model.

A user account should be associated with:

- Firebase UID
- User type
- School ID, where applicable
- Related student or staff ID, where applicable
- Account status
- Assigned roles
- Permissions

Recommended user types:

```text
SUPER_ADMIN
INTERNAL_STAFF
SCHOOL_ADMIN
PRINCIPAL
TEACHER
NON_TEACHING_STAFF
PARENT
STUDENT
```

These are proposed role identifiers, not proof that all roles are already implemented.

### Role examples

| Role | Permitted access |
|---|---|
| Super Admin | Authorized platform-wide oversight |
| Internal Staff | Assigned internal operations |
| School Admin | Authorized school-level management |
| Teacher | Assigned classes, subjects and students |
| Parent | Explicitly linked children |
| Student | Own permitted records |

Permissions must be enforced by trusted backend rules and services, not just by hiding buttons in the frontend.

### Critical rule

A frontend route guard is not a security boundary.

Firestore Security Rules and trusted backend validation must enforce the actual permissions.

Do not weaken rules to make a demo login or test dataset work.

